    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "18-23",
      "detail": "4th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Andrés Giménez turned Rogers Centre into his personal launch pad Monday night - two homers, five RBIs, a career first - yet the Blue Jays still crumpled <strong>5-8 to the Rays</strong>, their second straight gut punch after that 14-1 blowout over the Angels three nights back. At 18-23, fourth in the AL East and 9.5 games back, Toronto's trajectory screams mediocrity - 12th in the playoff pecking order, hemorrhaging ground to a Rays club that's won nine of 10 divisional tilts, first in the East to do so since these very Jays in '92. Giménez, now second on the team with 23 RBIs, is the spark in a lineup begging for consistency, while Max Scherzer, Shane Bieber, and José Berríos fester on the IL, leaving Kevin Gausman to preach patience amid the wreckage. The bats showed fight, clawing to within three, but defense and the pen betrayed them again. Short sentences. No miracles. <strong>Game two tonight against Tampa at 6:07 p.m. - Patrick Corbin on the hill - offers no mercy; sweep the series or sink deeper into the divisional abyss.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 12, 2026"
      },
